Hosting Rooms means securing three separate things: the web address people
open, the room server their sessions join, and the routes two peers use to
talk directly. Decide each one on its own. Putting the page behind HTTPS
does not make every peer route reachable, and changing how direct messages
travel does not change how sessions are shared.

# Choose the shape

For local development, `zig build rooms-web` serves the page on your machine
and runs the room server over encrypted iroh in a temporary data directory
with nothing minted. `zig build rooms-dev` keeps state under
`.local/.state/rooms` and mints every admin capability. A server that should
keep its identity and settings names its own directory:

```sh
GLOWCAP_ROOMS_STATE=/var/lib/glowcap/rooms \
  zig build rooms-web
```

To host it, install the complete glowcap product and Docker with Compose. The
deployment runs the page, an iroh room server, one durable session service, and
a PostgreSQL database for kept room lines. The product image contains the
glowcap and Rooms runtimes, prepared browser assets, skills, and iroh library.
It has no checkout or compiler and mounts no build output at runtime. The
database is reached only over the stack's private network. Its password is
stored in the deployment state directory and passed as a Docker secret.

```sh
zig build rooms-deploy-prep -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe -Dcpu=baseline
glowcap apps rooms deployment up --build "$PWD/zig-out/product"
```

The first `up` resolves and saves an immutable image ID, creates the shared
network and database secret, refreshes the managed Compose file, waits for
health, and prints the room locator and capabilities. Later starts reuse that
image. To select a tag, digest, or local image explicitly, pass `--image`.
The default image is the versioned GHCR name derived from the installed CLI;
this repository does not claim that the image has been published.

Deployment state lives in `glowcap/rooms-deployment` under `$XDG_STATE_HOME`, or
under `$HOME/.local/state` when that is unset or empty. Pass `--dir` to select another
configuration directory. It selects credentials and configuration, not another
stack: container, network, and volume names are fixed per Docker daemon. Keep
operator settings in `docker-compose.override.yml`, which `up` preserves.

The example override publishes the page on host loopback. Put an HTTPS reverse
proxy or private network in front of it for broader access. A loopback HTTP
address does not restrict the separately authenticated iroh directory.

The deployment front also has `doctor`, `status`, `logs`, `down`, and `reset`.
`doctor` is read-only. `down` keeps data. `reset` names a custody unit and
confirms before destroying it. Mutating commands lock the configuration
directory until they exit.

On a tailnet, Tailscale Serve can terminate HTTPS and forward the local
Rooms port. For a public server, use the container guide and an ordinary
TLS proxy. The
[deployment README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/deploy/README.md)
lists every environment variable and health check.

# Choose how people reach it

The room server's transport decides how sessions reach it:

| Transport | Use it when |
| --- | --- |
| iroh | The default. Encrypted, the peer proves who it is, and it finds a path through most NATs. |
| onion | You want a Tor onion service. The service key lives in the data directory, so the address survives a restart. |
| TCP | You are developing on your own machine. It is unencrypted and proves nothing about who answered. |

The server prints an address you can paste only after it has opened its
listener and loaded its saved identity. If that identity cannot be used, or
another process already owns the data directory, the server stops instead
of quietly minting a new one.

# Make sessions reachable

The web server carries bytes between a session on the page and the room
server over a WebSocket on the same address as the page. A rendezvous route
lets another peer call that session through the relay. The encrypted connection is still
between the two peers: the relay forwards bytes and holds nothing of theirs.

Direct messages over WebRTC need ICE servers. STUN helps two peers find a
direct route, and TURN carries the traffic when a network will not allow
one. Rooms can read a fixed ICE configuration, or ask a TLS service for
short-lived TURN credentials without ever giving the page that service's
secret.

If two peers exchange an offer but no route can be found, switching direct
messages to the relay usually brings the conversation back. It does not
change **Share session** or **Attach session**. A shared session advertises
its own routes, so trouble there is reported on its own.

# Run the session service

On its own, a session lives on one device. To open the same session, with
the same identity, from anywhere, run the session service:

```sh
glowcap apps rooms session serve --dir=.local/.state/rooms
```

The command prints a workspace link. Open it from the Session tab in Settings.
The service keeps the rooms, conversations, drafts, saved capabilities and
history choices in that workspace. Attaching opens the service's workspace;
it does not copy the browser's previous session into it. Reloading or opening
it on another device restores the same committed intent.

Management is separate. Its link is in `control.sturdyref` in the state
directory. Hold that link in **Session access** to create access for a person
or device, change its permissions, pause it or revoke it. The new workspace
link grants only the use you select. You keep its management control and can
share a separately limited management link. Reading history and files have
their own permissions.

What a link may do is checked in the session every time it is used, never
written into the link itself. So you can narrow a link, give it the earlier
messages or take them away, and pause it while someone is holding it, and
the link never changes. Someone holding a paused link is told that it is
paused and why. Revoking a link cuts off every open connection using it,
and it stays revoked after the service restarts.

# Keep the data directory together

Only one process may use a Rooms data directory at a time. Copy or restore
it as a whole. It holds the server's saved state, the record of unfinished
work, its identity, the randomness it publishes with, bridge settings, and
the credentials meant to persist. A partial copy would pair old settings
with a new identity, and the server cannot repair that, so never copy a
piece of it.

The session and archive services have data directories of their own, with
the same rule: one process, copied as a whole, and stop the service before
you move it.

# Decide who keeps history

Three places can keep history:

- The server can keep the last lines of each room for people who arrive
  later.
- A session can keep room and direct-message history on the device it lives
  on.
- An archive service can keep messages and files beyond any device or
  server.

History on the server follows the limits you set. History in a session or
an archive follows what each member chose. A shared archive link can be
read without letting the reader write to it or administer it.

Whether history lives in files or in PostgreSQL, the same limits per room
and per line apply. A write is acknowledged before Rooms treats it as saved.
A last record that was cut short is repaired when the journal shows what
came before it, and damage that cannot be put in order safely stops
recovery rather than guessing.

# Hand out admin links

Diagnostics, listener administration, peering, moderation, IRC settings, and
the live wire view are each a separate thing you can hold. Starting the
server with one of those features enabled mints a secret for it, and knowing
the web address alone gives you none of them. Paste a secret into the
Holdings tab in Settings and the matching page appears under `/ops`.

[The server page under /ops, opened with the admin secret. It lists what the admin can change: room links, registered names, live connections, and the references each one holds, with a remove or disconnect button beside every row.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-operations-dark.webp'))

[The server page under /ops, opened with the admin secret. It lists what the admin can change: room links, registered names, live connections, and the references each one holds, with a remove or disconnect button beside every row.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-operations-light.webp'))

The live wire view sends what happens on the wire to ocapnviz. A server can
show full room traffic. A person's direct messages show only who talked to
whom, when, which call it was, and how many bytes, never the bytes
themselves, because the other person never agreed to let a viewer read
their messages.

The full
[operating guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/operating.md)
covers each flag, the saved files, the PostgreSQL schema, the IRC forms, and
how to set up the live view.

# Read failures as they are

The deployment tells apart a service that refused to start, one that stopped
after it was ready, and a cleanup that also failed. Docker restarts services
according to its Compose policy, while `status` and `logs` show the current
state and reason.

Your session follows the same rule. A request still in progress
stays marked in progress, a known refusal offers the next step for that
code, and a request nobody can confirm is never shown as cleanly undone.

# Check a deployment

The focused test suite for the services is:

```sh
zig build rooms-peers-test
```

The browser tests drive Chromium through joining, connecting, reconnecting,
direct messages, files, WebRTC, shared sessions, archives, and the admin
pages. The
[development guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/developing.md)
lists each scenario and what it needs installed. Start with
`zig build rooms-web-page`, which runs one whole conversation in a browser.
